# Code owners for the release path and the package identity (REL.0C, Gate C G1).
#
# This file has NO effect on its own. It only routes review requests. It becomes a control when the
# ruleset (or branch protection) on main requires a pull request AND "Require review from Code
# Owners" - a human setting, see docs/operations/release-security-setup.md. GitHub never lets the
# author of a pull request approve it, so with a single maintainer that requirement needs a second
# identity; read that document before turning it on.
#
# Later patterns win. The catch-all comes first; the entries below it name the paths that matter
# most for releases, so they stay owned explicitly if the catch-all ever changes.

# Everything
*                                           @pedroloyola

# Workflows, dependency automation and this file
/.github/                                   @pedroloyola

# Release tooling, the version floor and the Store listing text
/tools/release/                             @pedroloyola
/release/                                   @pedroloyola

# What the build and the package are: SDK pin, MSBuild imports, project files, manifests
/global.json                                @pedroloyola
/Directory.Build.targets                    @pedroloyola
/src/Directory.Build.props                  @pedroloyola
*.csproj                                    @pedroloyola
/src/ServerMonitor.App/*.appxmanifest       @pedroloyola

# Architecture guards: tests that encode security and boundary invariants
/tests/**/Architecture/                     @pedroloyola
